meta {
  name: Refunds
  seq: 4
}

docs {
  Giving money back, and the three ways that must not go wrong.

  A refund is the one operation where a bug costs the shop money directly rather than
  costing it a sale, so each request here asserts what moved rather than that something
  returned 200.

  **The folder buys its own order.** It could lean on the one the Checkout folder leaves
  behind, and then it would only run as part of a full pass. Five requests of setup buys
  the ability to run `bru run Refunds -r` on its own while debugging.

  What it proves, in order: a partial refund moves the money and writes exactly one ledger
  row; the same idempotency key replayed returns the first refund rather than issuing a
  second; a refund larger than what is left is refused rather than clamped; the remainder
  can then be taken; and a cancellation refunds the whole outstanding balance and puts the
  units back on the shelf as one act.

  **The ledger, not the running total, is what is asserted.** `refunded` is a column that
  concurrent handlers can each write the same correct-looking value into. One row per
  movement of money is the fact that survives a race, which is why every check below counts
  `refunds.length` as well as reading the figure.
}
